d Eagle**: The rabbit needs the bald eagle.\n10.**Tiger Chases Bear**: The tiger chases the bear.\n11.**Tiger Green**: The tiger is green.\n12.**Tiger Sees Bald Eagle**: The tiger sees the bald eagle.\n13.**If Young and Red Then Green**: If something is young and red, then it is green.\n14.**If Sees Bear Then Needs Tiger**: If something sees the bear, then it needs the tiger.\n15.**If Needs Bald Eagle Then Chases Rabbit**: If something needs the bald eagle, then the bald eagle chases the rabbit.\n16.**If Big and Chases Rabbit Then Sees Bear**: If something is big and chases the rabbit, then it sees the bear.\n17.**If Sees Rabbit and Needs Tiger Then Tiger Not Blue**: If something sees the rabbit and needs the tiger, then the tiger is not blue.\n18.**If Sees Bald Eagle Then Needs Tiger**: If something sees the bald eagle, then it needs the tiger.\n19.**If Chases Rabbit Then Sees Bald Eagle**: If something chases the rabbit, then it sees the bald eagle.\n20.**If Rabbit Not Young and Rabbit Does Not Need Bald Eagle Then Rabbit Sees Bear**: If the rabbit is not young and does not need the bald eagle, then the rabbit sees the bear.\n21.**If Red and Needs Bear Then Big**: If something is red and needs the bear, then it is big.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the statement "The bald eagle is big":.\n\n- From point 16, we know that if something is big and chases the rabbit, then it sees the bear.\nSince the rabbit is red, the bald eagle is red, and the bald eagle is big, we can conclude that the bald eagle is not big based on the logical relationships provided. Therefore, the statement "The bald eagle is big" is false.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
